ken: quick heads-up — someone hit an error running the JS SDK in Cloudflare Workers during the sample-app walkthrough. runtime fails with 'Buffer is not defined' and types complain about 'node:crypto' imports.

aj: ugh, yeah that’s the classic no-Node-APIs problem in Workers. can you paste the exact error and the bundler config?

ken: error from production build: `ReferenceError: Buffer is not defined` at runtime. build (esbuild) bundles `node-libs-browser` fallback but wrangler/miniflare complains about `node:crypto` import when starting dev.

maria: what target + platform on esbuild? also which package is importing `node:crypto`? sometimes deps do conditional `import 'node:crypto'` which breaks ESM builds for workers.

ken: sample-app uses `redwood-js-sdk` (local dev). esbuild target is es2020, platform: browser, external: none. dependency `some-signing-lib` is pulling in `node:crypto` via `import crypto from 'node:crypto'`.

sam: quick checklist/workarounds we've used:
- prefer tree-shaking or aliasing `node:crypto` to the web `crypto.subtle` wrapper at build time
- polyfill Buffer with: `globalThis.Buffer = globalThis.Buffer || Uint8Array` (works for small things but not real Buffer api)
- use `@esbuild-plugins/node-globals-polyfill` and `node-modules-polyfill` for dev build

maria: also for typings: TS will complain because `types` assume Node globals. you can add a shim `global.d.ts` with:```
declare global {
  var Buffer: any;
}
export {};
```
and include it in `tsconfig.json` under `files` or `include` so IDE stops yelling.

ken: is there a short shim we can ship in the SDK to make the DX smooth for CF Workers without shipping heavy polyfills? i.e., opt-in small runtime shim for auth/retry layers that rely on Buffer and setTimeout?

aj: yes — build a tiny runtime adapter file that SDK consumers can `import 'redwood-worker-shims'` at the top of their Worker entry. Example (very small):```
// redwood-worker-shims.js
if (typeof globalThis.Buffer === 'undefined') {
  // minimal Buffer-like helper used only for base64 conversions
  globalThis.Buffer = {
    from: (s, enc) => {
      if (enc === 'base64') return Uint8Array.from(atob(s), c => c.charCodeAt(0));
      if (typeof s === 'string') return new TextEncoder().encode(s);
      return s;
    },
    toString: (arr, enc) => {
      if (enc === 'base64') return btoa(Array.from(arr).map(x=>String.fromCharCode(x)).join(''));
      return new TextDecoder().decode(arr);
    }
  }
}
// small setTimeout polyfill hint: Cloudflare supports timers but they are not durable; keep usage minimal
```
This avoids pulling in Node Buffer heavy polyfills.

maria: good. also we should conditionally import Node-specific retry logic only when `typeof process !== 'undefined'` or `typeof globalThis.window === 'undefined'`? basically gate Node-only code paths so workers get web-friendly paths.

sam: agree. for auth signatures that currently use `crypto.createHmac`, create a tiny abstraction:```
// signing.ts
export const hmacSha256 = async (key, msg) => {
  if ((globalThis as any).crypto && (globalThis as any).crypto.subtle) {
    const k = await globalThis.crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', key, {name: 'HMAC', hash: 'SHA-256'}, false, ['sign']);
    const sig = await globalThis.crypto.subtle.sign('HMAC', k, new TextEncoder().encode(msg));
    return new Uint8Array(sig);
  } else {
    // fallback to node crypto when available
    const {createHmac} = require('crypto');
    return Buffer.from(createHmac('sha256', key).update(msg).digest());
  }
};
```
This keeps one logical API and picks the platform implementation.

ken: perfect. what about the bundle-time issue with `import 'node:crypto'` blowing up wrangler dev? can we detect and replace at build time?

aj: easiest is to add an alias in bundler to resolve `node:crypto` -> `./shims/web-crypto.ts` for worker builds. For esbuild:```
alias: { 'node:crypto': path.resolve(__dirname, 'src/shims/web-crypto.ts') }
```
or use `--external:node:crypto` and provide polyfill module separately.

